      "problem": "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $(-2,0).$",
